WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.004
Your payment code expects one shape.

2
00:00:05.004 --> 00:00:09.547
charge, with an amount, a currency and a token, gives back a reference.

3
00:00:09.547 --> 00:00:11.083
That shape is your port.

4
00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:14.204
The issuer's SDK has another shape.

5
00:00:14.204 --> 00:00:19.431
It takes an XML string, answers in XML, and throws IOException.

6
00:00:19.431 --> 00:00:21.091
You can't change it.

7
00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:22.738
Push them together.

8
00:00:22.738 --> 00:00:23.873
They don't fit.

9
00:00:24.167 --> 00:00:28.990
So put a piece in between, with your shape on one side and theirs on the other.

10
00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:30.516
That piece is an adapter.

11
00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:31.810
Follow one call.

12
00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:37.847
Your amount, currency and token go in, and the adapter writes the XML the vendor wants.

13
00:00:37.847 --> 00:00:43.051
The answer comes back as XML, and the adapter hands you only the reference.

14
00:00:43.267 --> 00:00:45.006
Errors get translated too.

15
00:00:45.006 --> 00:00:50.491
The vendor throws IOException, and the adapter throws your own GatewayUnavailable.

16
00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:53.352
What if the adapter let IOException through?

17
00:00:54.733 --> 00:00:59.844
Then your code depends on the vendor, and a new issuer means changing every caller.

18
00:00:59.844 --> 00:01:03.045
That's a leaky adapter, the most common adapter bug.

19
00:01:04.133 --> 00:01:09.262
In Java, the port doesn't declare IOException, so javac won't let it escape.

20
00:01:09.262 --> 00:01:14.323
Kotlin has no checked exceptions, so the same leak compiles without a word.

21
00:01:15.367 --> 00:01:16.942
Now a different problem.

22
00:01:16.942 --> 00:01:21.929
Checkout is three steps: reserve the stock, charge the card, send a receipt.

23
00:01:22.133 --> 00:01:30.221
The REST resource, the Kafka consumer and the admin tool each make those calls themselves, and each gets the order a little different.

24
00:01:30.221 --> 00:01:34.627
One charges before it reserves, and takes money for stock you don't have.

25
00:01:34.833 --> 00:01:37.064
A facade gives them one front door.

26
00:01:37.064 --> 00:01:41.716
Each caller makes one call, checkout, and the steps move behind the door.

27
00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:43.680
The facade owns the order.

28
00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:46.692
Reserve, then charge, then send the receipt.

29
00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:49.347
And it owns what each failure means.

30
00:01:49.347 --> 00:01:52.951
If the card is declined, it releases the reservation.

31
00:01:54.233 --> 00:01:54.812
The rule.

32
00:01:54.812 --> 00:01:59.120
An adapter makes a class you can't change fit your port, both ways.

33
00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:03.107
A facade gives callers one door, and owns the steps behind it.

34
00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:04.120
Quick check.

35
00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:07.196
The charge fails after the stock is reserved.

36
00:02:07.196 --> 00:02:09.315
Which class releases the stock?

37
00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:12.111
The facade.

38
00:02:12.111 --> 00:02:14.471
The callers never see the steps.
